#!/usr/bin/env sh
# ^ This is a "shebang" line. When a file is executable and run directly,
# the operating system reads this exact line to pick the interpreter for
# everything below it. `#!` marks it as a shebang, not a plain comment;
# `/usr/bin/env` searches your PATH for the named program; `sh` is the
# POSIX shell (a basic, universally-available command interpreter). So
# this line says "run the rest of this file as a shell script."
#
# --- What is this file? (context for every file in .kamal/hooks/) ---
# This app is deployed with Kamal (kamal-deploy.org): a tool that builds a
# Rails app into a Docker container image and ships it to remote servers
# over SSH, without needing Heroku or Kubernetes. A deploy moves through a
# fixed sequence of stages, and at specific points in that sequence Kamal
# runs a "hook" — an executable script in this .kamal/hooks/ directory
# named after the stage it belongs to — if one exists.
#
# Kamal manages traffic routing to your app containers using a small
# built-in reverse proxy called "kamal-proxy," which normally gets
# restarted ("rebooted") as part of a deploy so it points at the new app
# version. This file is the "pre-proxy-reboot" hook: Kamal runs it once
# per target server right before kamal-proxy is rebooted there.
#
# The ".sample" suffix means Kamal will NOT run this file as-is. Kamal
# only runs a hook when a file with the exact stage name exists (here,
# "pre-proxy-reboot", with no ".sample") and is executable. This file is
# just a template/example. To activate it for real, copy it to
# "pre-proxy-reboot" (drop ".sample"), edit as desired, and make it
# executable.

# Blank line — no effect on execution; just visual separation between the
# comments above and the script logic below.

# `echo` prints the given text to standard output, which Kamal captures
# into its deploy log. The text is inside double quotes, so the shell
# variable written within it gets "interpolated" — replaced with its
# current value — instead of being printed literally.
#
# `$KAMAL_HOSTS` reads the KAMAL_HOSTS environment variable, which Kamal
# sets before running this hook to the comma-separated list of server
# addresses/hostnames targeted by this deploy. So this line would print
# something like: "Rebooting kamal-proxy on 1.2.3.4,5.6.7.8..."
echo "Rebooting kamal-proxy on $KAMAL_HOSTS..."
